            "type": "reasoning.text",
            "text": "These two currently-passing allowlist entries are interesting:\n- `this-is-not-detached.js`: `$DETACHBUFFER(detached)` then `detached.sliceToImmutable(start, end)` must throw TypeError. But sliceToImmutable is undefined in Node 26! `detached.sliceToImmutable(...)` would throw TypeError (\"not a function\"). So it passes, but for the WRONG reason (undefined method). This is a shallow allowlist entry that passes incidentally.\n\n- `species-returns-immutable-arraybuffer.js`: uses `arrayBuffer.sliceToImmutable()` — again undefined → TypeError. But the test expects a TypeError after `calls.push(\"Symbol.species(8)\")`. Wait, calls would be pushed first then sliceToImmutable throws → TypeError propagates. Actually the flow: `arrayBuffer.slice()` (built-in Node slice) → species ctor called → returns `arrayBuffer.sliceToImmutable()` which throws TypeError (not a function). That TypeError propagates out of slice → assert.throws(TypeError) catches it. And calls = [\"Symbol.species(8)\"]. So it passes incidentally too (because sliceToImmutable throws TypeError).\n\nSo these two entries pass for the wrong reason — they're \"report-only accidental\" passes. They're not a real implementation.\n\nSo E19.85 genuinely needs the immutable APIs implemented. Given the impossibility of faithful JS polyfill for typed-array write enforcement, this is a genuinely hard item.\n\nWait — but maybe Node CAN do immutability if I use `Object.freeze` on the ArrayBuffer? No — freezing an ArrayBuffer doesn't make typed array writes throw; typed array writes bypass property descriptors.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. Is it truly impossible? The spec's immutability means `new Uint8Array(immBuf)` creates a typed array whose [[Set]] throws. In pure JS, typed array writes are not interceptable. So on Node, we cannot produce an immutable ArrayBuffer via JS.\n\nGiven this, a faithful implementation is impossible for the js target. But the ROADMAP only lists `tests/test262` + runtime as tests, and says \"Target js only for v1\" for Test262.\n\nGiven the skill guidance \"If blocked on tools (e.g. system LLVM), set blocked and note the reason\" — this isn't quite a tool block, but it's a fundamental platform capability gap: Node doesn't support immutable ArrayBuffers and pure JS cannot emulate it. \n\nHmm, but is that really the intent? Let me reconsider. Maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me reconsider what tests can genuinely pass, and implement a polyfill that supports the *reachable* semantics: byteLength, .immutable getter, transferToImmutable/sliceToImmutable returning buffers with correct byteLength/maxByteLength, resizable:false, detach source, argument coercion, not-a-constructor, prop-desc, TypeError on shared/wrong-receiver. \n\nThe tests that require write-enforcement on immutable buffers (transfer/this-is-immutable-arraybuffer.js, typed array writes) would NOT pass — but they're separate files under ArrayBuffer/prototype/transfer etc. The E19.85 scope is the three specific methods. Let me check whether any of the immutable/, transferToImmutable/, sliceToImmutable/ files require write-enforcement (typed array writes to immutable buffer).\n\nLet me grep those three dirs for writes to typed arrays over immutable dest.\n\nLet me check each test file. Let me grep for `[` assignment or `=` write in those dirs.",
