c's DetachArrayBuffer, if already detached, returns). So making it idempotent is MORE correct. \n\nBut changing wrap_host_api affects other things. Let me consider — is that in scope for E19.85? The harness breakdown: the wire-up for detachArrayBuffer is in lib.rs wrap_host_api. I can make it idempotent: `if (buffer.detached) return;`. That only affects the detached case, making it a no-op — matches spec. Low risk.\n\nActually wait, would that break the E19.64 test `harness_detach_buffer`? That test detaches a fresh buffer — still works. And any test that calls $DETACHBUFFER twice... unlikely. So making it idempotent is safe and more correct.\n\nSo plan: in wrap_host_api detachArrayBuffer, add early return if buffer.detached.\n\nNow, the real issue remains the not-a-constructor and the method length/name. Let me rewrite polyfill to:\n- Use method-syntax (non-constructable) for transferToImmutable & sliceToImmutable, with explicit length set.\n- Getter via get syntax (non-constructable, name \"get immutable\").\n\nLet me also reconsider: `immutable/prop-desc.js` failed in runner due to my verifyAccessorProperty being wrong, but in the real harness verifyPrimordialAccessorProperty is robust. Let me not worry about runner prop-desc; trust real harness. But I should ensure the getter name is \"get immutable\" and length 0. Method/accessor syntax handles that.\n\nAlso not-a-constructor uses `isConstructor` from real harness (returns false for my method functions). And `new fns.transferToImmutable()` throws \"not a constructor\". Good with method syntax.\n\nNow let me also double check `new-length-coercion.js` \"did not throw\" — need to find root cause. It's likely a real polyfill bug. Let me instrument. Let me re-run with method-syntax polyfill and fixed compareArray, and add per-assertion stack. Actually new-length-coercion failed with \"transferToImmutable(1) did not throw\" — not a compareArray issue. Let me figure out which assert.throws. The badLengths include `[Symbol(\"1\"), TypeError]` and `[Symbol.for(\"1\"), TypeError]` and `[BigInt(1), TypeError]`. For Symbol: toIndex(Symbol) → toIntegerOrInfinity(Symbol) → Number(Symbol) throws TypeError (cannot convert Symbol to number). Good, throws TypeError.\n\nFor BigInt: toIndex(1n) → Number(1n) = 1, toIntegerOrInfinity=1, 1 in range → no throw! But test expects TypeError for BigInt. Per spec, ToIndex(BigInt) — ToIntegerOrInfinity(1n)? ToNumber of a BigInt... actually ToIndex: ToIntegerOrInfinity(value) where ToNumber(BigInt) — hmm, ToNumber on BigInt throws TypeError. Let me recall: `Number(1n)` in JS throws TypeError (\"Cannot convert a BigInt value to a number\"). Yes! Number(1n) throws TypeError. So toIndex(1n) → Number(1n) throws TypeError. Good.\n\nBut wait the failing message was \"transferToImmutable(1) did not throw\" — that's number 1, not in badLengths. Hmm, \"1\" appears... Actually the message uses `formatSimpleValue(rawLength)` = \"1\". Which rawLength=1 case expects a throw? In badLengths, none is plain 1. But the valueOf-object tests in badLengths: `[−1, RangeError]` → formatSimpleValue(-1) = \"-1\" not \"1\". \n\nHmm \"1\". Wait maybe it's the object coercion where valueOf returns `{}` (badValueOf) and toString returns rawLength... no that's good path.\n\nActually, maybe the issue is in my runner harness `assert.throws` catching the wrong thing, or the polyfill returned instead of throwing for some specific value. Let me just instrument new-length-coercion by adding a trace. Let me eval it with a wrapper that logs each assert.throws call.\n\nActually — simpler: the message format is \"transferToImmutable(\" + formatSimpleValue(rawLength) + \") did not throw\". Let me just add a stack trace to assert.throws in the runner and re-run new-length-coercion with method-syntax polyfill. Let me first rewrite polyfill with method syntax, then re-run everything with corrected compareArray.",
