      "output": "<path>/Users/jaredhembrow/Projects/draconic/draconic/CONTEXT.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Draconic\n2: \n3: Draconic is a programming language: a full ECMAScript superset with TypeScript-inspired static types and native systems types, compiling to JavaScript and to native binaries via LLVM.\n4: \n5: ## Language product\n6: \n7: **Draconic**:\n8: The language itself — source programs, their meaning, and the surface syntax developers write.\n9: _Avoid_: the compiler, “the project”, the repo name\n10: \n11: **Program**:\n12: A unit of Draconic source that the toolchain accepts as input (file or string).\n13: _Avoid_: script, module (unless meaning ECMAScript Module), codebase\n14: \n15: **Native type**:\n16: A static, unboxed systems type (e.g. `i32`, `i64`, fixed structs) that is not a JavaScript language type.\n17: _Avoid_: primitive (ambiguous with JS primitives), POD, rust type\n18: \n19: **JS value**:\n20: A heap-managed value with JavaScript semantics (objects, arrays, strings, closures, etc.).\n21: _Avoid_: dynamic value, any, object (when meaning the whole heap universe)\n22: \n23: **Dual worlds**:\n24: The coexistence of JS values and native types in one program, with explicit boundaries at the type and lowering level.\n25: _Avoid_: FFI-only model, “just typed JS”\n26: \n27: ## Toolchain\n28: \n29: **Toolchain**:\n30: The whole Draconic product a developer runs: compiler, runtime, embed, and CLI.\n31: _Avoid_: compiler (when meaning the whole product), SDK\n32: \n33: **Compiler**:\n34: The Rust program that turns Draconic source into artifacts (JS text or native object/binary), not including the shipped runtime process itself.\n35: _Avoid_: tsc, transpiler (when the native path is in view)\n36: \n37: **Frontend**:\n38: The compile path from source (or a filesystem entry) through parse, bind, typecheck, and lower into IR. Script vs Module (link) policy lives here; callers do not wire stage crates.\n39: _Avoid_: parser (when meaning the whole front half)\n40: \n41: **Linker**:\n42: Loads an entry path’s ESM import graph, mangles bindings, and flattens to one Program. Owned by `draconic-linker`; Frontend chooses parse vs link. Not part of the Parser product.\n43: _Avoid_: bundler (when meaning our static link step), parser\n44: \n45: **IR**:\n46: The shared intermediate representation both backends lower from after the Frontend.\n47: _Avoid_: AST (post-check), bytecode (unless a concrete IR form is bytecode)\n48: \n49: **JS backend**:\n50: The lowering from IR to ECMAScript source (or equivalent) with semantic equivalence where the target can express the program.\n51: _Avoid_: emitter, transpiler, tsc\n52: \n53: **LLVM backend**:\n54: The lowering from IR through LLVM to a native binary (or object), linked with the Runtime.\n55: _Avoid_: native backend (prefer this only as informal shorthand), codegen\n56: \n57: **Runtime**:\n58: The native support linked into binaries: GC for JS values, async job queue, standard library hooks, and the Embed path.\n59: _Avoid_: VM (unless a true bytecode VM is meant), libc\n60: \n61: **Embed**:\n62: The compiler (or equivalent) shipped inside the Runtime so `eval`, `new Function`, and similar can compile source at run time on the native target.\n63: _Avoid_: interpreter (unless that is the chosen eval strategy), JIT (unless tiered compilation is meant)\n64: \n65: ## Types and checking\n66: \n67: **Checker**:\n68: The static analysis pass that assigns and validates types (TypeScript-inspired, not tsc-compatible).\n69: _Avoid_: typechecker as a product name, tsc\n70: \n71: **Portable program**:\n72: A Program that both backends can accept with equivalent observable behavior (after documented polyfills).\n73: _Avoid_: isomorphic, universal\n74: \n75: **Native-only** / **JS-only**:\n76: A feature or Program that is valid on exactly one backend; the other must hard-error with a diagnostic, never silent wrong code.\n77: _Avoid_: unsupported (too vague)\n78: \n79: ## Completeness process\n80: \n81: **Roadmap**:\n82: The ordered feature checklist that defines what remains for language completeness; source of truth with the test suite.\n83: _Avoid_: backlog, kanban, tickets (for this loop)\n84: \n85: **Conformance suite**:\n86: Tests that pin ECMA-262 and native-type behavior; a Roadmap item is done only when its tests are green on the applicable targets.\n87: _Avoid_: unit tests (when meaning the suite as truth), spec tests (ambiguous)\n88: \n89: **Loop**:\n90: One atomic iteration of the mega-loop skill: pick the next Roadmap item, red-green it, mark done only when tests pass.\n91: _Avoid_: sprint, agent session (session may host one Loop)\n\n(End of file - total 91 lines)\n</content>",
